            "Lesson 1.1 defined evidence-based practice in David Sackett's words as the conscientious, explicit and judicious use of current best evidence in making decisions about the care of individual patients, and insisted on its three legs: the <strong>best available research evidence</strong>, the nurse's <strong>clinical expertise</strong> — her trained judgement about this patient, this ward and what is feasible here — and the <strong>patient's values, preferences and circumstances</strong>. Remove any leg and it falls. Evidence without judgement becomes cookbook nursing that applies a trial of Scandinavian intensive care patients to a Tamale medical ward; judgement without evidence is ‘we have always done it this way’, which is still the commonest reason given on a ward for routine wound irrigation with antiseptic, routine shaving before surgery, routine enemas in labour and routine episiotomy, each of which the evidence overturned decades ago; and evidence and judgement together without the patient produce the perfectly justified plan the patient does not follow. Evidence-based practice is therefore not a rule that only randomised trials count, nor a device for managers to cut costs, nor the enemy of experience — it is a disciplined way of letting what has been found change what is done, patient by patient, and its five steps have come to be called the five As: <strong>ask</strong> a focused question, <strong>acquire</strong> the evidence, <strong>appraise</strong> it, <strong>apply</strong> it to the patient and the setting, and <strong>assess</strong> the result.",
