            "The uncertainty arrives as a worry — ‘I think we turn these patients too rarely’, ‘the matron says honey heals ulcers faster’, ‘should we be giving zinc with the ORS?’ — and becomes answerable when it is written in the four-part form of lesson 1.2: population, intervention, comparison and outcome, with the type of question identified (therapy, harm, diagnosis, prognosis, experience), because the type decides which design would answer it best and therefore what to search for. ‘In adults with venous leg ulcers, does honey dressing compared with standard saline dressing shorten time to healing?’ is a therapy question, and the best design is a randomised trial or a systematic review of trials. Acquiring the evidence then starts at the <em>top</em> of the pyramid rather than the bottom: not a single trial found on Google but the <strong>pre-appraised</strong> sources where someone has already found, assessed and combined the studies. A <strong>systematic review</strong> searches for every study on a question by an explicit, reproducible method — registered protocol, several databases, grey literature, no language limit — appraises each against stated criteria and synthesises them; a <strong>meta-analysis</strong> goes further and combines their results statistically into one pooled estimate with a confidence interval, displayed on a <strong>forest plot</strong> whose diamond at the bottom is the summary and whose horizontal lines are the individual trials, with a <strong>funnel plot</strong> to look for the publication bias of lesson 3.1. The <strong>Cochrane Library</strong> is free in Ghana through HINARI and holds reviews on most nursing interventions; <strong>clinical practice guidelines</strong> from the World Health Organization, the Ghana Health Service and the <strong>Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines</strong> translate reviews into recommendations graded by the strength of the evidence behind them; and only when these are silent does the nurse descend to PubMed, CINAHL and the primary studies. A guideline, though, is only as good as its method, and is itself appraised: who wrote it and with what conflicts of interest, whether it was based on a systematic search, whether each recommendation is linked to the evidence and its grade, when it was last updated, and whether it was developed for a setting like ours — the AGREE instrument asks these questions in a checklist.",
