            "A <strong>research proposal</strong> is the written plan of a study before it is done, and it is read by three audiences: the supervisor and examiners who must agree that it is worth doing and can be done, the ethics committee that must agree it is safe and fair, and — first and most usefully — the researcher herself, because writing the plan is how she discovers, cheaply and before any participant's time is spent, that the question is two questions, that the sample cannot be reached, that the instrument does not exist, or that the analysis she imagined needs data she had not planned to collect. Ghanaian nursing schools organise the proposal as the first three chapters of the eventual project, and each section has a question it must answer. The <strong>title</strong> names the variables, the population and the setting in about fifteen words, with the design where it helps (‘Exclusive breastfeeding and early return to work among mothers attending a municipal child welfare clinic in Ho: a cross-sectional study’). <strong>Chapter one</strong> is the introduction: background to the problem, the problem statement with its figures (lesson 1.2), the research questions or hypotheses, the aim and objectives, the significance of the study — who will use the answer and how — the operational definitions, and the scope and delimitations. <strong>Chapter two</strong> is the literature review organised by theme and ending in the gap, with the conceptual or theoretical framework — a diagram of how the variables are thought to relate, drawn from a nursing or behavioural theory or built from the review — that the analysis will later follow. <strong>Chapter three</strong> is the methodology, and it is written in enough detail that another nurse could repeat the study: design and its justification; study setting described; study population, inclusion and exclusion criteria, sampling method and the sample-size calculation shown; variables and instruments with their sources, validity and reliability; the pilot; the data collection procedure step by step, including who collects, where, when and in what language; the data management and analysis plan, naming the software and, objective by objective, the descriptive statistics and the tests (lesson 6.2); the ethical considerations — committee, consent, confidentiality, risks and benefits, data storage; and the limitations anticipated. After the chapters come the <strong>work plan</strong> — a Gantt chart of the months from proposal defence to submission, with ethics approval allowed eight to twelve weeks — the <strong>budget</strong> (stationery, printing, transport, data collectors' allowances, participant reimbursement, airtime, software, dissemination), the <strong>references</strong> in the required style and the <strong>appendices</strong>: information sheet, consent form, instruments in every language used, letters of introduction and permission. A proposal written only to pass a defence tells the panel what it wants to hear; one written to test the idea tells the researcher the truth, and is then the one the panel passes.",
