          alt: "Growth monitoring: the direction matters more than the dot. Reading the weight chart, age in months along the axis: this child is falling away — a line that flattens or turns down is the warning, even inside the normal band. MUAC in children 6 to 59 months: under 11.5 cm red, severe; 11.5 to 12.5 cm yellow, moderate; 12.5 cm or more green, adequate; swelling of both feet is severe malnutrition whatever the MUAC or the weight says; MUAC needs no scale, no power and no height board. Why a child is weighed every month, and what the weighing is for: the purpose is not the number; it is to catch the child who is beginning to fall, while it is still cheap to fix, and to give the mother a reason to come, so that immunisation, vitamin A and counselling reach her too. A weight recorded and not acted on is not monitoring; ask what changed after the last weighing. Weigh, plot, interpret, explain to the mother, then act — most charts stop after plot. Vitamin A 100,000 IU at 6 months, then 200,000 IU every six months from 12 to 59 months; deworming from 12 months six-monthly; zinc for diarrhoea 10 to 14 days. Every contact is an opportunity; check the card for what is missing, not only for why she came.",
