            "<strong>Dissemination</strong> is the last step of the research process and the one most Ghanaian projects never take: the bound copy goes to the library, the findings go nowhere, and the mothers, nurses and districts who gave their time receive nothing. Publication in a <strong>peer-reviewed journal</strong> is how findings enter the literature where lesson 1.2's next student will search for them. The manuscript is submitted to one journal at a time — the Ghana Medical Journal, the African Journal of Nursing and Midwifery, the International Journal of Africa Nursing Sciences, BMC Nursing and others, chosen for scope, indexing and a fee the author can afford or have waived — and the editor sends it to two or three <strong>peer reviewers</strong>, experts who read it anonymously (or openly) for sound methods, honest results and justified conclusions, and recommend acceptance, revision or rejection; the author revises with a point-by-point response, and the process takes months. Peer review is imperfect — it misses fraud and some errors — but it is the filter that separates the journal from the predatory outlet of lesson 4.2, and a reviewer's criticism is free supervision. Beyond the journal, findings travel as a <strong>policy brief</strong> of two pages for the district director or the regional health directorate — the problem, the finding, three recommendations, in plain language with one figure; as a <strong>presentation</strong> to the facility's staff and the district health management team, who can change practice next month; as a <strong>summary in the local language</strong> read at a durbar or posted at the clinic for the community that took part; as a conference abstract; and, increasingly, as a short thread or video for colleagues online, with the same care for confidentiality as any other publication. The researcher's responsibility continues after all this: she acts on what the study found in her own practice; she answers the facility that asks how to implement the recommendation; she reports to the ethics committee that the study is closed; she keeps the data and consents for the required period; and she corrects the record if an error is later found. Research is also <strong>continuing professional development</strong> — the Nursing and Midwifery Council awards credits for presenting, publishing and attending research training, and the nurse who completes a study and disseminates it has earned a year's requirement and, more to the point, has become the colleague on the ward whom others ask ‘what is the evidence?’",
