          paragraphs: [
            "<strong>Quality improvement</strong> borrows from industry the idea that most failures are produced by the system rather than by the individual, and that the system is improved by small, rapid, measured changes rather than by an annual policy. Its tool is the <strong>Plan–Do–Study–Act cycle</strong>. <strong>Plan</strong>: state the problem in numbers from the baseline data (hand-hygiene compliance before aseptic procedures 42 per cent in 60 observations), set an aim with a number and a date (80 per cent within three months), and choose one change to test, predicted to help (alcohol hand rub mounted at every bed rather than at the sink). <strong>Do</strong>: try it small — one bay, one week, one shift — and record what happened, including the surprises (the dispensers were emptied by visitors). <strong>Study</strong>: measure again the same way and compare with the baseline and the prediction; plot the measurements over time on a <strong>run chart</strong>, because a single before-and-after pair cannot distinguish a real shift from the ordinary week-to-week variation that lesson 6.1 described, and a run of six or more points above the baseline median can. <strong>Act</strong>: adopt the change across the ward if it worked, adapt it if it half-worked, abandon it if it did not, and go round again with the next change. Three measures are followed at once: the <strong>outcome</strong> measure (pressure ulcer incidence), the <strong>process</strong> measures (repositioning documented, risk assessment completed) that show whether the change is actually being done, and the <strong>balancing</strong> measures that show whether something else got worse (staff overtime, falls while turning). Improvement that is not measured is opinion; measurement that is not acted upon is audit without the cycle; and the staff nurse with a run chart on the wall and three completed cycles has more authority at the management meeting than the one with a complaint.",
          ],
