          alt: "Patient flow: find the bottleneck, not the blame. A queue is a symptom; it forms at the slowest step, and it never forms where people are working hardest. Steps: arrival and registration; vital signs and triage; consultation with two clinicians — the queue forms here; laboratory; pharmacy and exit. Speeding up any other step changes nothing: adding a second registration clerk moves patients to the consultation queue faster; the waiting room is calmer nowhere and the total wait is unchanged; only the constraint sets the throughput of the whole system. How to find it: walk the patient's route yourself, with a watch; record the time at every step for twenty patients; the step where people accumulate is the constraint, whatever the staff at each step believe. Discharge is a flow problem too: a ward is full at ten in the morning because yesterday's discharges leave at four in the afternoon; plan the discharge on admission, not on the morning it happens. Before adding staff or money to a service, find out where the work actually stops moving. Most queues are caused by a decision nobody is available to make, not by a task nobody has time to do.",
