            "Lesson 5.2 explained how the data a nurse enters each month become the district's indicators, and lesson 3.2 explained why they are often wrong; this lesson closes the loop by putting them to work where they are collected. A sub-district whose staff complete the monthly returns, send them to the district and never see any analysis has learned that the forms are for someone else, and the completeness and accuracy of its data fall; the <strong>data review meeting</strong> — monthly at the facility, quarterly at the district — reverses this. The facility's own indicators are pulled from DHIMS2 and the registers: outpatient attendance by diagnosis; antenatal registrants, fourth visits and the proportion booking before 16 weeks; deliveries, stillbirths and neonatal deaths; Penta1, Penta3 and the dropout rate; malaria tests performed and the proportion positive; family planning acceptors; the ward's admissions, deaths, mean and median length of stay, bed occupancy, pressure ulcer and fall counts; the stock-outs. Each is plotted <strong>month by month on a line graph on the wall</strong>, with the previous year beside it and the target drawn across — because the trend matters more than the dot (lesson 7.2), and because a line that climbs from 18 malaria cases to 40 over three weeks is the epidemic curve the surveillance officer needs to see before the laboratory confirms anything. The staff ask of every indicator the questions this course has taught: out of how many; over what period; who was left out; is this a rise in disease or a rise in testing and reporting; is the denominator the census projection or the register; what changed last month — a stock-out, a new nurse, a public holiday, a rumour; and what will we do about it, by whom, by when, written in the minutes and checked at the next meeting. The ward's monthly data become the baseline for the next audit and the run chart for the next improvement cycle; the sub-district's data become the evidence that persuades the district to send a second midwife; and the rise in pressure ulcers that would have appeared in the annual report eleven months late is seen in the month it begins.",
